>> title: Chopped Liver
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: chicken liver, onion, schmaltz, spread, deli
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: chopped chicken liver, gehakte leber, jewish chopped liver, liver spread
>> pairs-with: schmaltz, deli-rye-bread, sour-dill-pickles
>> servings: 8
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: unforgiving — cooked liver is a two-day item in the fridge whatever the bowl looks like, and the livers go grey and chalky past six minutes a side
>> step.1: boil the eggs 11 min, ice them
>> step.2: fry the onions 25 min in schmaltz
>> step.3: broil the livers 6 min a side
>> step.4: chop coarse with schmaltz
>> step.5: chill 2 hr

Boil @eggs{4%large} in @water{2%qt} for ~boil{11%min}, then cool them under cold water and peel them. Two go into the bowl and two are held back to grate over the top, which is how the case sends it out.

Fry @yellow onions{2%large}(sliced thin) in @schmaltz{3%Tbs} over medium-low heat and ~fry{25%min}, until they are brown and sweet all the way through, not merely soft. This is where the flavour of the dish actually lives; onions taken off pale make chopped liver taste of nothing but liver.

Broil @chicken livers{1%lb}(450 g; trimmed of every green spot and sinew) seasoned with @kosher salt{1%tsp} on a rack close to the element, ~broil{12%min}, turning once, until they are firm and no longer red in the middle. Broiling rather than frying is the traditional method and it is not fussiness — it is how the blood is drawn out, and it gives a drier liver that chops instead of smearing.

Chop @&(~1)broiled livers{} together with @&(~2)fried onions{} and @&(~3)two peeled eggs{} in a wooden bowl with a hand chopper, working in @schmaltz{2%Tbs}, @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} until it holds together but you can still see what it is made of. A food processor turns the whole thing into pâté in four seconds — that is a real thing and it is on this site as pork liver pâté, but it is not this.

Chill @&(~1)chopped liver{} ~chill{2%hr}, then grate the two held-back eggs over the top and serve it with rye and raw onion. It keeps two days, and the flavour is best on the first.
